st infamous of these white powder  events was the amerithrax attacks in the  fall of 2001  that left 17 people sick and five dead  from anthrax sent via anonymous letters  containing white powders since these  attacks the fbi and u.s postal service  has  responded to thousands of white powder  events more often than not the attacks  do not contain any toxic material  and are carried out for the sole purpose  of causing terror and damaging  infrastructure  these fake bioterrorist agents consist  of white powders  that can come from a variety of uh  different commercial sources  and household products and they may be  blended to further complicate analysis  and they really do happen all the time  and have a variety of targets  they also don't often occur in isolation  as a perpetrator may send letters over a  long period of time  such as in this case where a texas man  sent over 500 white powder letters over  a period of six years  and the targets can range from  politicians to the news media  but we know that nobody really cares  unless it affects celebrities and that  they are targeted as well  so the research we did uh we we looked  at uh  three very commonly available artificial  sweeteners  which to protect proprietary information  they're called  the pink stuff the yellow stuff and the  blue stuff but i think you know what i'm  i'm speaking about  and all of these artificial sweeteners  contain a very small  or trace amount of a sweetening agent  um in the case of the pink stuff at  saccharin the yellow stuff it's  sucralose and the blue stuff uses  aspartame  and then they have a bulking agent which  is the dextrose um in all all three of  them  and this usually um can consist of  or takes up anywhere from 95 to 99  of the whole sample therefore when you  try to analyze these with a bulk  analysis  you're only going to get the dextrose  it's almost impossible to see the  um the sweetening agent  because of just the sheer quantity of  the dextro  so here's um some of the data we have  and uh this shows  that the sweeping agents all are very  different rom and spectra which allow  them to be differentiated very trivially  using this method and this analysis  i had a one second acquisition time and  you can really see the quality of the  raman spectra here  now we need to find those particles  though  from the dextrose and what this shows  is the overall particle size  distribution of the blend so showing  uh all the components within each of the  mixtures and you can see that they're  very similar  um and so uh differentiating these  samples  difficult if not impossible  and when you look at the bulking agent  again  it's the same thing because the dextrose  is the majority of the sample  you're really seeing um that's really  the main contributor to the overall  particle size distribution  however when you look at the  just the identified sweetening agent um  you can see that they're now different  so now not only can they be  differentiated um  well not only can they be found these  you know needles in a haystack  and identified by their ramen spectra  but their particle size distributions  are also very different  and so this really can help um this  method can  really help identify and differentiate  the different artificial sweeteners  from each other in an automated method  now at two artificial sweeteners with  the same ingredients so they both  contain sucralose and dextrose  but one is the name brand and the other  is a generic  and we were wondering if you could tell  the difference um between them using  morphologically directed raman  spectroscopy  so here again shows just the nutritive  dextrose and the distributions of  of their particle sizes are the same  so you wouldn't be able to tell these  apart either by their particle sizes  or their distributions or their chemical  identification  however when you look at the sweetening  agent you can see that the particle size  distributions are very different  so this is a case where the chemistry  isn't going to tell them apart  however their particle size and  morphologies are very different  and so you would be able to  differentiate the generic from the name  brand  using this method  now another problem we tackled was  looking at  both illicit and counterfeit drugs uh  the world  uh drug report by the united nations  estimated  that about 269 million people or  505.3 percent of the world's population  have used a drug  at least once in 2018. and drug use  continues to be on the rise  now it's not just the common drugs like  marijuana and cocaine we're now seeing  designer drugs such as bath salts and  the  opioid epidemic and it's really a huge  problem that just continues to grow  we also have a problem with counterfeit  drugs um and the sale  of counterfeit drugs is estimated to  reach  um over uh 1.2 trillion dollars per year  as of 2018 as well and this has to do  with the growing availability of the  illegal pharmacies online  and morphologically directed ramen  spectroscopy was used  to identify the components of these drug  mixtures  and more than just that it's also able  to potentially provide comparative  source attribution  so the first thing we did was we made a  mixture of  equal parts by weight of five different  common drugs and  this was a very easy problem for mdrs to  identify all components and give us both  the  morphological information about each  component  and the size distributions and particle  size distributions  so mixtures are not a challenge at all  for the sam uh this instrument  we also looked at bath salts now bath  salts are designer drugs of abuse that  used to be available legally you could  buy them in a  gas station or bodega they're now  illegal and they are generally central  nervous  system stimulants and can lead to a  variety of serious and potentially  lethal reactions  in bath salts is mdpv although there are  many other stimulants that might be  present  so this is one example that i purchased  through a website and  it came back with a three competition  mixture  the first component that was identified  with l-dopa that's  a and it seemed a little strange that  that was  uh present but um it was um the second  component was unidentified  although it uh its structure was similar  to a phenethylamine so it could have  been  a bath salt or a catheter that was not  in our spectral library and then the  third component  identified with caffeine and that's not  uncommon to be found in bath salts since  it is intended to be used as a stimulant  and so um we were able to get  information  on the chemical identity of the mixtures  components as well as their morphology  and particle size distribution which  potentially could be used  um for comparative uh purposes  mdrs can also be used for  over-the-counter drugs  and also then compared to a suspected  counterfeit  when we're talking about  over-the-counter drugs we're dealing  with  highly regulated materials so their  particle size distributions were  incredibly reproducible  and you can expect a counterfeit drug um  wouldn't be made in exactly the same way  wouldn't have the same  quality assurance and so the uh the part  even though it generally might contain  the same ingredients because  um counterfeiters want you to rebuy the  material  um there are substandard qualities that  could be detected  with morphologically directed vomit  electronically  uh identif we um contaminated  a sample of uh dextromor uh  dextromethorphan  uh with baking soda just in a one to one  thousand ratio  um and we were still able to find the  baking soda within  the gxm and that was very exciting to  see how  sensitive this method really is  another thing we looked at was soil  forensic soil analysis is one of my  favorite  topics i think it's incredibly  underutilized evidence  it is one of the most traditional  methods it's been used for  over a hundred years and as han grove  said  dirt on shoes can often tell us more  about where the wearer of those shoes  had last been than toil some inquiries  people collect dirt on their  and people don't often think to clean it  off or even if they do clean it off  it might not be incredibly thorough and  a forensic scientist is able to look at  the soil  and examine it using microscopy and  other methods  um and compare the the soil from a shoe  or a shovel or  a a tire tread and compare it to a  source to see if they could have come  from  the if the sample could have come from  that location  one of the most useful properties of  soil that is used for comparison  is the mineral identification and  although there are more than 2 200  minerals are known in existence there  really  are only about 40 that are commonly  encountered in most soils  so um when it comes to traditional  forensic soil analysis  we've been using particle particle size  distributions and mineral  identifications  uh for a long time because uh and  they've been shown to be very useful  comparative features for soil analysis  and so using either ndrs or pcrs for  this analysis  because it combines the two is really  ideal  so here's some results of of one of the  analysis we did  we looked at soil samples uh that we  collected from  uh four sites along a single road in  connecticut  um the mineral portions were separated  out through  washing and um and sieving to get  a certain particle size fraction the the  90 to 180 micron size fraction and when  we analyzed the soil minerals  um in each of those samples there was a  couple surprises  one is um that we had more than than  four components that we were able to  find  not in great amounts but there were  really small amounts  of certain minerals that ended up being  very useful  and um even though these uh  soil samples were within  a short close proximity to each other  they were all able to be distinguished  um based on the presence or absence of  certain minerals so for example sample  site a  contained um epidote  in a certain quantity that was different  than  than other samples or sample c  had adopted dioxide which wasn't present  in any of the other samples  we did  sorry so we also did a principal  component  analysis on the data you can't have a  forensic science presentation nowadays  without some  multivariate statistical analysis  and we did pca principal component  analysis  and you can really see the the  separation of the four different  locations  was really great  um the last type of evidence we looked  at  with mdrs was gunshot residue  gunshot residue is really one a very  common  type of evidence because it's easily  transferred and gun crimes are very  common especially in the united states  the most widely accepted method for  gunshot residue identification is  scanning electron microscopy equipped  with energy disperses  x-ray spectrometry or sem eds  and this method what makes it so  uh useful is that it combines both the  morphology  of the um gunshot residue particles with  the elemental chemistry so you're  looking  for that spherical ball um  and using edf you're going to identify  the  lead barium and antimony components  now if you find these particles if you  find three or more then  it's a positive identification for  gunshot residue  however there is a a big push in the  forensic science community to find  alternative methods um there are a  number of reasons for this  um one of which is uh because lead-free  ammunition or green ammunition it's  really on the rise  and therefore we wouldn't be able to use  the lead bearing  antimony from the primer to make the  identification  now one of the the tools that's being  heavily investigated is the use of raman  spectroscopy for gsr identification  because this can combine both the  organic  and inorganic components of gut jet  residue for identification  one thing that traditional ramen is  missing though is that morphology  so when the primer um is  uh hit and the uh and  put to through the barrel  the primer is vaporized and then it  condenses  back into the little spherical ball so  that's really what's characteristic  is this molten ball of primer  part primer material and so  morphologically directed raman  spectroscopy would be  potentially able to combine that  morphology information  with the analytical chemistry  and what we did was we um  collected uh four test fires at various  distances  and we were able to get both the  particle size  information and particle morphologies  and identify different components  so this shows the spectra of the  nitrocellulose  that we saw and one of the the trends  that we saw that we  was a little bit unexpected  appeared with the muzzle to target  there there was a relationship where the  larger particles  were seen in the um the closer  test-fired  samples and this really does need to be  investigated more  but it's interesting to think that the  morphology could give us information on  muscle to target distance  so some overall conclusions to  particle-driven raman spectroscopy  methods  are that they're non-destructive  relatively fast they're automated  ways to collect chemical and particle  size information about  a range of samples of forensic interest  and  we used this to analyze a range of  traces  um that and we believe that this has the  potential to  enable criminalists to obtain more  information from forensic evidence  than is currently employed for  investigations and adjudications  so one last um tool  typical photothermal infrared plus raman  spectroscopy  and with this method um  opt-ir is really can be described as a  pump  probe optical spectroscopy technique  where the pump is an infrared laser  which excites the sample  uh commonly a qcl laser and the probe is  either a 532 or 785 nanometer laser  and this is what allows for the  simultaneous infrared and raman  analysis also because this technique  uses a visible or near-infrared laser  probe  submicron spatial resolution is possible  and therefore you can get raman-like  spatial resolution with infrared  spectral information  and last although this is a reflection  technique  the spectra that are obtained are really  like transmission and so the spectra are  clean there's no distortions or  artifacts or interference fringes  and they can be searched in traditional  infrared libraries  so this shows a video of how optical  photothermal infrared works  so this uh and i'm going to take you  right through it  it starts with an infrared light probe  which is a pulse source  being incident on the sample now in the  infrared  the diffraction limit is very large  usually 10 microns or greater  the co-linear visible beam can focus  much narrower  at about 500 nanometer spot size  at the surface of the sample the energy  is absorbed  and you get localized heating and  thermal expansion that changes the  refractive index  and sample deformation which then  changes how the green light is scattered  it then measures the green light  intensity as  we tune the infrared wavelengths of the  probe  and this results in a pure absorbance  infrared spectrum that can be compared  to traditional infrared transmission  so there are some key differentiators of  opt-ir to traditional  ftir microscopy um you get  a sub-micron spatial resolution um  you don't have to make contact with your  sample so  you it it operates in reflection and  there's no dispersive  scattering artifacts we don't need to  use an infrared detector  um so no longer do i have to buy my  liquid nitrogen  um you also because it's a surface  technique we don't need to microtome it  to make  thin sections um it can work on any type  of uh  thick section or thin you also don't  have to worry about the substrate it's  on  it works on glass or any other  transparent substrate  and then the the best part about it is  that it collects both infrared and raman  spectra from the same spot at the same  time  with the same spectral resolution  so i used it to analyze paint evidence  and  paint very common evidence we get it  submitted to forensic labs in the form  of automotive paint architectural paint  spray paint  and also in the case of arteries or  or in the case of forgery for artwork  um and the beauty of paint is that it's  highly complex  and there's a huge variety of it so  there's a tremendous variety of colors  and the colors change year to year uh  trends change so believe it or not in  the 1980s red  was one of the most popular colors  whereas now  white is still the most popular color  and has been for about  uh for more than a decade  and the main components of paint um are  the binders solvents  pigments extenders and additives so it  really is  a very complex mixture and not only is  it  that it's applied on a surface so oem  automotive paints  usually have three or four layers you  can have a primer and a primer surfacer  and then a color coat and then a clear  coat but there  there's variations to this as well so  there's a lot of variations which  provides  for its evidentiary significance  we have um standard methods  for analyzing paint and forensic science  uh specifically astm  1610 which was updated in in 2018  um and it shows a flow chart for  examining the physical and chemical  characteristics of automotive paint  and this continues the flow chart and  one thing that you can see  is that in to identify all three  components the binders the pigments or  the additives  you are able to use vibrational  spectroscopy it's an incredibly valuable  but there are and when we analyze those  um materials using traditional ir and  raman microspectroscopy  we do have some limitations so first is  a limited spatial resolution which i  mentioned before  infrared is usually 10 microns ramen is  less  you can get you know a 1 micron spot  size  but with the infrared spectra only going  to about 10  and we have some layers that are are  less than that  it makes it very difficult if not  impossible to get the infrared spectra  of  a sample that with a layer that's less  than 10 microns  it often requires very complex sample  preparations  um total reflection has become very  popular  um it's one of my favorite techniques to  use but it does require intimate contact  um and then with raman spectroscopy  although it has the spatial resolution  and doesn't require contact  um one of the the issues that uh that's  very  problematic is fluorescence  opti won't correct for that but it's  just something we have to live with  so in this is an example of an  automotive paint that we analyzed  um and you can see how how nice the  infrared spectra are  and how um they were able to be  um so we used an infrared a hyper uh so  it shows an optical image and then the  mirage infrared hyperspectral image  overlaid on top of it  and from this information  on the spectra obtained we were able to  identify  um all of the layers of the paint here  by comparing it  to published literature by scott ryland  and ed suzuki  and so the top coat was an oxaphane  the green mid or color color coat was an  acrylic urethane  and then the primer was an acrylic  urethane  modified with melamine and this shows  the ramen spectra  um now to note that the the color code  gave a really beautiful  ramen spectra that enabled us to  identify the pigment as a blue  thiocelenide pigment  here shows the ramen hyperspectral image  that was collected  um and showing it just the the 5 15 20  peak which is from the blue dial  the halo cyanide pigment and really this  could be used  to compare different uh paints because  you can see the distribution of the  pigments within the color code  this is another sample and in this case  we did a line array  which saves a little bit of time and we  got  similarly fantastic infrared spectra  in a matter of minutes um we also  um did mirage ratio imaging which  um allows us to look at the ratio of  different peaks  and which highlights chemical  differences of the layers within a paint  sample  now one thing that's particularly  interesting to me in this is that  it showed a mixing of certain  chemistries at the boundaries  between the two middle layers now this  is only a two micron region  and it isn't uh seen at every wave  number so it's unknown why this is  happening  uh perhaps they used a wet on wet baking  process or one of the layers wasn't  fully dry prior to applying the second  layer  or even potentially there was a  diffusion over time of some of these  chemical components  now although it's not currently used for  for  foreign comparisons um this mixing  at the boundary layer may prove to be  potentially valuable for discrimination  in comparison  this shows the raman hyperspectral image  and the last  uh thing i wanted to show was we did a  comparison of two  red automotive paints that were  indistinguishable by uh microscopy  and you can see that they're very  similar  in appearance now ramen spectroscopy  didn't help us here  um now although uh both samples did  fluoresce  um they fluoresce differently so um  you know it does tell us that they are  potentially different but there's not a  lot of satisfaction  uh doing a comparison just based on  fluorescence  because we weren't able to to really  make any identifications  so looking at different spots on these  two paints we were able to get really  beautiful infrared spectra  that enabled us to do a comparison  um and we looked at also the single  frequency imaging  and one of the things that we did  was delay four uh ratios  and what i find really fascinating here  is that  the effect pigments in the color code  are you can really see the morphologies  of these so  not only are you able to identify the  components and look at the chemistry  but the morphology of the of the  material and the pigments which  um can be seen visually but now  chemically are being mapped and that's  really amazing in my book  so getting back to the chemistry uh the  opt-ir analysis  uh showed real um you know some subtle  differences between the two  and when we uh did a infrared spectral  interpretation uh although both clear  coats contained acrylic melamine enamel  there were differences in the chemistry  of all the three other coats that  enabled these  uh two red paints um to be  clearly distinct  one another so  in conclusion um so new developments in  instrumentation uh specifically with  morphologically directed raman  spectroscopy particle correlated raman  spectroscopy and optical photothermal  infrared  plus raman spectroscopy will enable more  information to be obtained  from the analysis of complex samples  than could be previously obtained  by morphological or chemical information  alone  so in my book it's really like uh one  plus one equals three here  and these tools can be used for a range  of forensic samples  i have a lot of people i collaborate  with and i'm i'm indebted  to all of them  i'd like to thank university of new  haven for supporting my research  and my collaborators at hariba at  malvern analytical at microtrace at john  jay college  ohio university and last but not least  is  a photo thermal which makes the opt-ir  and if there are any questions please  feel free to to reach out by email  i'm happy to answer any questions anyone  has  and thank you for attending
 

